@task
Title: Feature retrospective, 5 August 2026
Status: Done
Owner: ...
@endtask

Charts from tables shipped. The syntax lets a table carry a chart argument naming the type, plus which column holds the labels and which hold the values. Add a row and the chart follows, and the table still renders underneath so the numbers stay readable.

The implementation was much smaller than expected because it turned out to be an adapter rather than a renderer. The existing chart drawing code already took a structure of labels and series, so converting a parsed table into that structure was about thirty lines per language. Only Go and JavaScript render charts, so only two implementations changed.

A deliberate decision: no new validation warnings. The five parse only SDKs implement semantic validation, so any new validate issue would have meant seven ports. Chart problems render as visible warnings beside the chart instead. The cost is that a typo in a column name gets no editor squiggle.

Reader side chart type switching was cut during implementation. Redrawing in the browser would need a third chart renderer beside the Go and JavaScript ones, which is the duplication the design was supposed to prevent. Choosing the type stays the author's job.

Two bugs were caught before release. The interactive runtime queried an attribute that no renderer emitted, so hover would have done nothing at all. And the folding configuration used a pattern that matched single line directives like image, which have no closing tag, so folding regions opened and never closed.

